Interview Questions & Answers

Q1. How does Snowflake calculate monthly storage costs?

Snowflake calculates storage costs in two steps:

Every 24 hours, Snowflake calculates the average amount of data stored that day across both database table data and internal stages
At the end of the month, Snowflake takes another average across all daily figures

That final monthly average (in terabytes) is then multiplied by a flat rate per terabyte. This averaging approach means adding or removing data mid-month is fairly accounted for rather than being billed on a snapshot at one point in time.


Q2. What are the two areas that contribute to Snowflake storage costs?

1. Database Table Data — this includes ALL stages of the data lifecycle:

Active micro-partitions (current data)
Time Travel micro-partitions (older versions retained for recovery)
Fail-Safe micro-partitions (7-day Snowflake-managed retention)

2. Internal Stages — any files stored in internal stages contribute to billing. Unlike table data, the user has direct control over stage files and should clean them up after loading to avoid unnecessary costs.


Q3. What three factors affect the per-terabyte storage rate in Snowflake?

The storage rate per terabyte varies based on:

Cloud Provider — AWS, Azure, or GCP
Region — e.g., AWS London vs AWS AP Mumbai have different rates
Pricing Plan — On-Demand (pay as you go) vs Capacity (pre-purchased, typically cheaper)

This means two accounts storing identical amounts of data can have very different monthly bills purely based on where their account is deployed and their purchasing plan.


Q4. Why can setting a 90-day retention period significantly increase storage costs?

Because ALL three stages of the data lifecycle contribute to storage billing. With a 90-day retention period:

Every time data is updated or deleted, the old micro-partitions are kept for up to 90 days in Time Travel storage
After Time Travel expires, 7 more days of Fail-Safe storage are added
For frequently updated tables, this means storing many versions of data simultaneously

The trade-off is powerful recovery capabilities vs significantly higher storage bills. It is good practice to set shorter retention periods on large, frequently updated tables and longer periods only on critical data.


Q5. Two accounts store different amounts of data but the second account pays more. How is this possible?

This is entirely due to regional pricing differences. For example:

AWS London: 15 TB × $42 = $630
AWS AP Mumbai: 14 TB × $46 = $644

The Mumbai account stores less data (14TB vs 15TB) but pays more ($644 vs $630) purely because the per-terabyte rate is higher in that region. This highlights the importance of region selection when setting up a Snowflake account, especially for large-scale data storage use cases.


Q6. How do internal stages contribute to storage costs and how can you manage this?

Any files uploaded to internal stages are billed as storage just like table data. Unlike table data (which Snowflake manages automatically), stage files are under the user's direct control. A common mistake is uploading large, uncompressed files to a stage and forgetting to delete them after loading — these continue to accumulate storage costs indefinitely.
Best practices:

Delete stage files immediately after a successful COPY INTO load
Use compressed file formats before uploading
Regularly audit internal stages for stale files
Set up automated cleanup processes after data loading pipelines



Q7. How do Transient and Temporary tables help reduce storage costs?

Both table types reduce storage costs by limiting or eliminating the data protection stages:

Transient tables — max 1-day Time Travel, NO Fail-Safe. Data passes directly out of storage after Time Travel expires, with no 7-day Fail-Safe retention
Temporary tables — session-scoped only. Automatically dropped when the session ends, with no Fail-Safe

For staging tables, ETL intermediary data, or any non-critical data, using transient or temporary tables instead of permanent tables can significantly reduce the storage bill.


Q8. What is the difference between On-Demand and Capacity pricing plans for storage?


On-Demand — pay as you go. No upfront commitment. Higher per-terabyte rate. Good for variable or unpredictable workloads.
Capacity — pre-purchased storage commitment. Lower per-terabyte rate. Better value for predictable, large-scale storage needs.

For example, On-Demand in AWS London is $42/TB. A Capacity plan for the same region would typically offer a lower rate, making it more cost-effective for organizations with stable, large storage requirements.


Q9. A company has a large table that is updated frequently and has a 90-day retention period. They want to reduce storage costs without losing all data protection. What would you recommend?

Several strategies can help:

Reduce the retention period on the large table specifically (child object overrides parent) — even dropping from 90 to 30 days significantly reduces Time Travel storage
Use Transient tables for intermediate or staging versions of the data — no Fail-Safe, max 1-day Time Travel
Evaluate which tables truly need 90-day retention — apply long retention only to the most critical tables
Clean up internal stages after every load cycle
Monitor storage consumption regularly using SHOW TABLES and INFORMATION_SCHEMA to identify the largest consumers


sql-- Check storage usage per table
SELECT TABLE_NAME,
       ACTIVE_BYTES / (1024*1024*1024) AS ACTIVE_GB,
       TIME_TRAVEL_BYTES / (1024*1024*1024) AS TIME_TRAVEL_GB,
       FAILSAFE_BYTES / (1024*1024*1024) AS FAILSAFE_GB
FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.TABLE_STORAGE_METRICS
ORDER BY (ACTIVE_BYTES + TIME_TRAVEL_BYTES + FAILSAFE_BYTES) DESC;

